**Acid reflux** is when **stomach acid comes back up** into your oesophagus (food pipe). When it happens regularly and causes symptoms or damage, it is called **gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD)**. It is very common.

## Symptoms

- **heartburn**, a burning feeling in the middle of the chest
- an **unpleasant sour taste** in your mouth
- bringing up food or liquid
- a cough or hiccups that keep coming back
- a hoarse voice
- bad breath
- feeling bloated or sick

Symptoms are often worse after eating, when lying down or bending over.

## Common causes

- certain **food and drink**, such as coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty foods
- being **overweight**
- **smoking**
- **pregnancy**
- stress and anxiety
- some medicines, such as ibuprofen
- a **hiatus hernia**, where part of the stomach moves up into the chest

{% callout type="warn" title="See a GP urgently" %}
See a GP if you have heartburn with:

- **difficulty swallowing**, or food getting stuck
- losing weight without trying
- being sick often, or vomiting blood
- black or tarry poo

You may be referred for an **endoscopy**, a test that uses a thin camera to look inside your food pipe and stomach.
{% /callout %}

Read about [treatment for acid reflux](/conditions/digestive-and-liver/acid-reflux/treatment/).
